    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** and **Taylor Ward** represent our offensive pillars, anchoring the lineup with scores of 63.41 and 60.20, respectively. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** is an auto-start despite a matchup against Warren, as his role at the top of the order provides a critical +8% boost, offsetting the modest difficulty of the opposing arm. Meanwhile, **Taylor Ward** benefits from an elite park factor (+16%) and the coveted leadoff spot, making him our highest-upside play against Hancock. While **Luis Arraez** isn't quite at the 60+ threshold, his 56.98 score is bolstered by a +6% order adjustment, keeping him relevant as a high-floor accumulator of counting stats against the favorable Lovelady.\n\nThe benching of **Jonathan Aranda** was the most difficult decision of the day; his 35.75 score fell well short of our 40.0 floor, primarily due to a weak SP skill matchup (-16%) against Early. Despite his solid underlying xwOBA, the combination of a tough pitching matchup and an unfavorable platoon split rendered him a liability compared to our starting middle infielders. We continue to monitor the status of **Yainer Diaz**, who remains sidelined on the IL, and we are managing our game caps carefully by keeping the rest of our bench, including **Lawrence Butler**, sidelined for this rotation.\n\nCaution is advised with the bottom of our order, specifically **Drew Gilbert**, whose 42.97 score sits precariously close to our \"below floor\" threshold. His placement in the nine-hole combined with a L/L platoon disadvantage against Lovelady minimizes his immediate scoring potential. We are locked in with the current configuration, but we remain vigilant regarding the volatile pitching of Lovelady, whose 4.66 xERA theoretically offers a path for **Jung Hoo Lee** and **Matt Chapman** to outperform their modest projections if they can capitalize on early-game mistakes.",
